1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:06,507
I'm Abhishek. This is Whodunit, built for
the Agents of SigNoz hackathon — Track 2,

2
00:00:06,587 --> 00:00:11,139
Signals and Dashboards. When production
breaks, every observability tool shows you

3
00:00:11,219 --> 00:00:16,184
the difference between the traces that
failed and the ones that didn't.

4
00:00:16,264 --> 00:00:20,816
BubbleUp, Trace Patterns, Grafana's
compare — they rank attributes and hand

5
00:00:20,896 --> 00:00:27,517
you a panel. Then a human re-types that
finding into a query, at three in the

6
00:00:27,597 --> 00:00:32,562
morning. Whodunit closes that loop. One
ClickHouse scan, mine the pattern that

7
00:00:32,642 --> 00:00:38,435
separates the cohorts, compile it into a
real SigNoz query, verify it against the

8
00:00:38,515 --> 00:00:43,894
live engine, and arm it as an alert. Five
stages, no LLM anywhere.

9
00:00:44,600 --> 00:00:48,738
And this isn't a problem I invented for a
hackathon.

10
00:00:48,818 --> 00:00:54,611
SigNoz's co-founder asked for exactly
this, three and a half years ago — still

11
00:00:54,691 --> 00:00:55,891
open.

12
00:00:56,300 --> 00:01:00,852
Here's the fault, drawn. A healthy
checkout calls the flag service.

13
00:01:00,932 --> 00:01:05,483
A failing one retries redis, and the
flag-service span is missing.

14
00:01:05,563 --> 00:01:09,701
But the retry alone shows up in healthy
traces too.

15
00:01:09,781 --> 00:01:13,506
And plenty of healthy traces skip the flag
check.

16
00:01:13,586 --> 00:01:17,723
Neither condition alone separates them —
only both at once.

17
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:24,252
One question to ClickHouse. Then the whole
lattice is enumerated locally.

18
00:01:24,332 --> 00:01:30,125
The machine weighs the obvious answers —
the edge alone — and rejects them.

19
00:01:30,205 --> 00:01:34,343
Only the conjunction separates the
cohorts, at thirteen times lift.

20
00:01:35,660 --> 00:01:40,626
This is what every flat tool sees —
BubbleUp, compare, Trace Patterns.

21
00:01:40,706 --> 00:01:45,257
Its best single predicate lands at
precision zero point one seven.

22
00:01:45,337 --> 00:01:48,234
The fault needs two conditions at once.

23
00:01:49,190 --> 00:01:55,397
No model wrote this. It's compiled into
SigNoz's own grammar — A implies B, and

24
00:01:55,477 --> 00:02:00,029
not C — then run back against the engine.
Mined sixty-one.

25
00:02:00,109 --> 00:02:01,350
SigNoz returned sixty-one.

26
00:02:01,450 --> 00:02:08,484
And this is why it has to be SigNoz.
Traces and logs live in one ClickHouse, so

27
00:02:08,564 --> 00:02:15,185
a single scan joins them by trace ID. On
Tempo plus Loki, that join doesn't exist.

28
00:02:16,550 --> 00:02:21,929
That permalink opens the machine-written
query in SigNoz's own Trace Explorer — all

29
00:02:22,009 --> 00:02:26,147
three leaves and the operator, rendered in
the real UI.

30
00:02:26,227 --> 00:02:29,538
The result set collapses to the matched
traces.

31
00:02:29,618 --> 00:02:34,997
Open one, and the flame graph shows the
shape: payment retries redis, and

32
00:02:35,077 --> 00:02:36,318
flag-service never ran.

33
00:02:44,550 --> 00:02:49,516
One flag arms it. The discriminator
becomes a native v2alpha1 alert with

34
00:02:49,596 --> 00:02:54,975
warning and critical thresholds. Replay
the fault, and at t plus one eighty-two

35
00:02:55,055 --> 00:03:02,089
seconds the webhook fires — the rule ID in
that payload is the rule the command just

36
00:03:02,169 --> 00:03:06,307
created. Everyone else in this category
stops at a panel.

37
00:03:06,387 --> 00:03:12,180
None of them hand you back a query, a
dashboard, and an armed tripwire.

38
00:03:15,210 --> 00:03:21,417
Here's what matters more. Point it at a
decoy — an attribute that tracks failure

39
00:03:21,497 --> 00:03:28,118
but doesn't cause it — and it abstains.
Point it at a healthy system, it abstains

40
00:03:28,198 --> 00:03:32,749
again. Across six seeded faults, it never
named a false culprit.

41
00:03:32,829 --> 00:03:39,036
And this one it first got wrong — it
abstained where a real answer existed.

42
00:03:39,116 --> 00:03:44,909
I found the seam, fixed it, and left the
original failure in the repo.

43
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:51,978
Run it twice. Same input, same seed, same
verdict hash.

44
00:03:52,058 --> 00:03:57,023
There is no LLM anywhere in the runtime —
this is deterministic.

45
00:03:57,103 --> 00:04:02,069
The corpus is synthetic and disclosed;
ground truth comes from a manifest.

46
00:04:12,940 --> 00:04:17,492
And you don't need my machine to check any
of it.

47
00:04:17,572 --> 00:04:23,779
The whole run is stepped through in the
browser — the board, the compiled query,

48
00:04:23,859 --> 00:04:27,169
the receipt, the hash — hosted, no
install.

49
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:35,192
Everyone can show you the difference. Only
SigNoz can arm it.

50
00:04:35,272 --> 00:04:40,651
Everything's on GitHub — one command
reproduces the whole run, casting file and

51
00:04:40,731 --> 00:04:41,931
lock committed.
